reateBox`, `createPadCursor`, `createDialog`, `createMenu`.\n\n**The store (WP 10.9)**: what a game keeps across sessions (high scores, progress, its settings) in the page's localStorage under the game's prefix, versioned and checked on load; memory in Node and in the isolated modes.\n`createStore`, `memoryBackend`.\n\n**Lights, shadows, atmosphere and sky (WP 7.1)**: light kinds for a level's light spots, a fixed pool of point lights near the focus with a shadow budget by quality tier, and sky presets (background, dome, backdrop, sun, ambient light, fog) that a sky puts on a scene.\n`defineLight`, `defineSky`, `createSky`; types `LightEntry`, `LightSource`, `SkyEntry`, `SkySpec`, `Sky`.\n\n**Materials v2 (WP 7.2)**: the same material data drawn in four styles (lambert, toon, flat, pbr), switched at run time with every style warmed; the toon look (bands and rim) as uniforms; outline shells for meshes and instanced batches.\n`createStyleSet`, `MATERIAL_STYLES`, `TOON_LOOK`, `createOutline`, `OUTLINE_MODES`; types `MaterialStyle`, `StyleSet`, `StyleSetOptions`, `ToonLook`, `ToonUniforms`, `Outline`, `OutlineMode`, `OutlineOptions`, `OutlineShells`.\n\n**Post-processing (WP 7.4, 13.32)**: one chain every frame draws through, every filter a uniform with an amount, scoped by part; looks as data (a material style, a filter chain, the sky, WP 7.8) that combine looks; objects tagged by part.\n`createPost`, `POST_SETTINGS`, `definePostSettings`, `setPart`, `PARTS`, `defineLook`, `FILTER_FIELDS`, `FILTER_NAMES`, `SCOPES`, `PALETTES`, `STYLIZE_KINDS`; types `Post`, `PostOptions`, `Part`, `PostTag`, `LookEntry`, `LookSpec`, `FilterName`, `Scope`, `FilterChain`, `FilterSpec`, `CombineLayer`, `PostShow`.\n\n**Props drawn (WP 4.5)**: every prop part a batch of the instancing service, posed by its body, the flames dancing on the visual streams, the light spots flickering, for the light pool; a part's geometry and where it stands (its matrix in the prop's frame), for a prop's tests.\n`createPropMeshes`, `FLICKER`, `buildPropPart`, `partMatrix`; types `PropMeshes`, `PropMeshesOptions`, `PropMeshPose`, `PropLight`.\n\n**Crowd rendering and pose LOD (WP 5.4)**: thousands of humanoids, blobs and floaters in a few dozen draw calls over the instancing service, each body seen through bands of detail by its distance (the animator's pose level, capes), culled by the view; the blob and floater bodies as data.\n`createCrowd`, `createCrowdLod`, `CROWD_LOD`, `BLOB_BODY`, `blobBody`, `FLOATER_BODY`, `QUADRUPED_BODY`; types `Crowd`, `CrowdDraw`, `CrowdRigDraw`, `CrowdStats`, `RigBody`, `RigPart`, `RigJoints`, `RigShape`, `CrowdLod`, `CrowdLodOptions`, `CrowdLodCounts`, `CrowdBand`, `CrowdBandSpec`, `BlobFeatures`.\n\n**Performance counters (WP 5.5)**: what `__engine.stats()` returns, means in ms and the renderer's counts, never fps.\ntypes `EngineStats`.\n\n**The texture library (v2, WP 7.3)**: a level's whole floor as a texture generator (define it under a game's id), with moss at the walls' feet, a rune circle on each dais and contact shadows along every block's foot.\n`levelFloorTexture`; types `LevelFloorOptions`, `ContactShadowOptions`, `FloorMossOptions`, `FloorRuneOptions`.\n\n**Clips in pages (WP 8.4)**: three.js's AnimationClip and AnimationMixer, for presentation only (a page plays a baked clip on a puppet with clipJSON and AnimationClip.parse); the sim samples the same tracks through the clip layer. The mannequin: the Quaternius figure the library was measured on, as a body and a build.\n`AnimationClip`, `AnimationMixer`, `defineMannequin`, `mannequinBody`, `MANNEQUIN_SLOTS`; types `MannequinData`.\n\n**Effects (WP 6.4)**: particles as data (emitters: shardfall's presets, the 2D engine's kinds, event effects), drawn with telegraphs on the floor, socket trails, bolts and camera shake by a view's effects; never part of the hash.\n`createEffects`, `FX_BUDGET`, `BOLT`, `defineEmitter`, `getEmitter`, `TRAIL_TINTS`, `ScreenShake`, `SHAKE`; types `Effects`, `EffectsOptions`, `FxEventPayload`, `FxStats`, `Emitter`, `EmitterSpec`, `EmitterLayer`, `ParticleFields`, `FxLight`, `BurstOptions`, `ParticleStats`, `TrailSampleLike`.\n\n**The feel kit (WP 13.4)**: courses, scripted runs of a scene and the numbers measured on them (`node x feel`); the runner and its verdicts; the owner's feel panel and copy as profile.\n`defineCourse`, `getCourse`, `listCourses`, `METRICS`, `runCourse`, `createFeelPanel`, `tunedSpec`, `profileText`; types `Course`, `Measure`, `Metric`, `CourseResult`, `CourseSample`, `MeasureResult`, `FeelPanel`, `FeelRow`, `FeelInspector`.\n\n**Camera modes (WP 2.5, WP 3.9, WP 13.8)**: a code to a camera, each mode's factory, and a game's own.\n`readCameraCode`, `createCamera`, `createShoulderCamera`, `SHOULDER`, `createFollowCamera`, `FOLLOW_RIG`, `createFirstCamera`, `createChaseCamera`, `createViewCamera`, `followCamera`, `defineCamera`, `makePose`, `poseForward`, `poseRight`, `poseUp`, `poseTarget`; types `AnyCamera`, `AnyCameraSpec`, `ShoulderInput`, `ShoulderSpec`, `FollowInput`, `FollowSpec`, `FollowCamera`, `FollowSteps`, `CameraKindSpec`, `Camera`, `CameraInput`.\n\n**The rig a view steps (WP 13.8)**: blends on live switches, the view effects, the view settings cam.*.\n`createCameraRig`, `rigOptions`, `CAMERA_SETTINGS`, `blendCameras`, `BLEND_TIME`, `createCameraEffects`, `applyEffects`, `cameraPlayer`, `apexFov`, `EFFECTS`; types `CameraRig`, `RigOptions`, `RigInput`, `CameraPlayer`, `EffectWeights`, `EffectsFrame`.\n<!-- api-map:end -->\n","numLines":479,"startLine":1,"totalLines":479}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016gWJmmmNB8q6abq8Gfxynk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjW7gBz2xFUS1oKzsBGm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015zVM8Bt9EVZWaumry7NT3v","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md CLAUDE.md DOCTRINE.md | head -80; find labs -maxdepth 3 | grep -v node_modules | head -100; wc -l docs/*; cat package.json | head -50","description":"Peek at workspace docs and starter files"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2211,"cache_read_input_tokens":63234,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2211},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9dd98077-2f1f-4b79-bf36-2031f176f317","uuid":"d61ff824-70a2-40f8-82e1-3b6bd5b962e2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T08:56:43.203Z","request_id":"req_011CgjW7fwMrT69gsPLszPrV","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_015zVM8Bt9EVZWaumry7NT3v":{"command":"cat ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md CLAUDE.md DOCTRINE.md | head -80; find labs -maxdepth 3 | grep -v node_modules | head -100; wc -l docs/*; cat package.json | head -50","description":"Peek at workspace docs and starter files"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015zVM8Bt9EVZWaumry7NT3v","type":"tool_result","content":"# Engine feedback: teeter\n\nWhat this game needed from the my-3dge engine (at 2dbd829) and did not get. The owner collects this file\nfrom every game to improve the engine, so write each item for an engine agent who has never seen this repository:\nwhat you tried, what happened (the command, the error code), what you expected, and what you did instead.\n\n## Requests\n\n<!-- What the engine should add or fix, most costly first. -->\n\n## Friction\n\n<!-- Docs, errors, templates or APIs that slowed you down or misled you, most costly first. -->\n\n## Local engine patches\n\n<!--\n`npm run check` accepts an edited engine file only when it is listed here, one line each, its path in backticks:\n- `engine/path/to/file.ts`: why no workaround in the game would do, and what the engine should change instead.\n-->\n@AGENTS.md\n\n# Claude Code notes\n\n- The SessionStart hook runs `bash scripts/setup.sh`; the after-edit hook formats and lints the file you edited:\n  fix what it reports.\n- When a replay misses its goldens or two runs part, docs/GAME-GUIDE.md section 7 says how to find why.\n# Agent-First Game Engine: Doctrine\n\n## How to read this\n\nRead this as a trusted, capable manager building an engine that you and others will use. We don't need to tell you to lock the door at night, keep company secrets off public message boards, avoid burning the place down, or get approval before spending money. Use your judgment. When you think going against this doctrine is best, escalate and explain your reasoning. Rules, principles need to be flexible and we have a process to allow for that.\n\n## North Star\n\nBuild a game engine designed from the ground up for AI coding agents (\"agents\"). Every layer is built so agents can develop, test, inspect, and debug it directly. We deliberately leave behind human-centric development tooling and any development method agents cannot use well. The result: games built faster, more consistently, and at higher quality than with general-purpose engines retrofitted for agents.\n\nAnalogy: build a fully autonomous fighter jet with no cockpit. Removing the pilot removes the constraints a human imposes on the design. Humans give direction remotely; agents fly the plane.\n\nOptimize for the best expected overall output, not flawless software. Solve problems when they actually surface, not in anticipation.\n\n## Principles\n\n1. Agent-readable\n   Code, data, and project structure are optimized for agent comprehension over conventions that serve only humans.\n\n2. Agent-operable\n   Every capability is exposed through machine interfaces. Nothing requires a GUI unless no other option.\n\n3. Verifiable without a display\n   Everything can be run, tested, inspected, and verified without a screen or a human watching.\n\n4. Agent-accessible assets\n   Source assets are created in this order of preference:\n   1. Procedural: code and data that generate the asset.\n   2. Text/data: formats agents read and edit directly.\n   3. Binary: only with human approval. Approved: fonts (WOFF2, TTF, OTF).\n\n   Rationale: agents work best with code and data, and are improving at that faster than legacy asset tools are improving for agents.\n\n5. Reproducible on the development platform\n   On the development platform, identical inputs and seeds produce identical gameplay state, and any gameplay state can be captured, restored, and replayed.\n\n6. WebGPU only\n   WebGPU is the only renderer, in a browser or through a native implementation (ie Vulkan). Gameplay runs only on the CPU. Game UI in HTML/CSS is allowed.\n\n7. Common ground\n   Everything agents work with when building games uses widely used languages, libraries, systems, and patterns. Favor flexibility, ease of use, and established approaches over cutting-edge performance and capabilities.\n\n   Analogy: build the Sherman, not the Tiger.\n\n   Rationale: lean into what agents already do well rather than trying to change it.\n\n8. Quality under the hood\n   Engine internals, meaning code whose API does not appear in game code, use the highest-quality approach their builder can execute well, however complex. Reuse what others have already built well, and invest heavily in what is ours. That complexity stays behind the API: agents need to use the engine well, not read or rebuild it, even when something goes wrong.\n\n   Rationale: the engine is built once and used many times, so better internals raise the quality of everything built on it.\n\n9. Mastery over novelty\n   Use the newest version that is at least 12 months old, so agents know it deeply. A newer version that is backward compatible with a qualifying version, or otherwise works the same way, is adopted immediately, since agents' existing knowledge still applies. Within engine internals covered by principle 8, the builder may use newer versions when they raise quality and it can use them well. Upgrade as newer versions qualify.\nlabs\nlabs/box\nlabs/game\nlabs/box/clipFiles.ts\nlabs/box/sounds\nlabs/box/touch.ts\nlabs/box/library.ts\nlabs/box/README.md\nlabs/box/index.html\nlabs/box/menuControls.ts\nlabs/box/cast\nlabs/box/menu.ts\nlabs/box/looks\nlabs/box/main.ts\nlabs/box/scenes\nlabs/box/levels\nlabs/game/best.test.ts\nlabs/game/best.ts\nlabs/game/README.md\nlabs/game/index.html\nlabs/game/cast\nlabs/game/main.ts\nlabs/game/replays\nlabs/game/scenes\nlabs/game/bot.ts\nlabs/box/sounds/boxHall.ts\nlabs/box/sounds/page.ts\nlabs/box/sounds/events.ts\nlabs/box/sounds/effects.ts\nlabs/box/cast/slime.ts\nlabs/box/cast/walker.ts\nlabs/box/cast/hero.ts\nlabs/box/cast/wisp.ts\nlabs/box/looks/torch-cel.ts\nlabs/box/looks/torch-lit.ts\nlabs/box/looks/torch-fxaa.ts\nlabs/box/looks/torch-pixel.ts\nlabs/box/looks/torch-bloom.ts\nlabs/box/looks/daylight.ts\nlabs/box/looks/hall-floor.ts\nlabs/box/looks/torch-toon.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/room-hero.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/ragdoll.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/hall.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/crowd-1000.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/fight-foes.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/hall-props.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/clips.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/fight-hero.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/crowd-100.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/crowd-5000.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/fight-rules.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/fight-1000.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/room.ts\nlabs/box/scenes/fight.ts\nlabs/box/levels/hall.txt\nlabs/box/levels/room.txt\nlabs/game/cast/player.ts\nlabs/game/replays/bot.replay.json\nlabs/game/scenes/play.test.ts\nlabs/game/scenes/play.ts\n  10757 docs/API.md\n   1506 docs/ERRORS.md\n    478 docs/GAME-GUIDE.md\n  12741 total\n{\n  \"name\": \"teeter\",\n  \"version\": \"0.0.0\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"description\": \"A game made with the my-3dge engine.\",\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"node\": \">=24.10 <25\"\n  },\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"vite\",\n    \"build\": \"vite build\",\n    \"typecheck\": \"tsc -b tsconfig.check.json\",\n    \"lint\": \"eslint --cache --cache-location node_modules/.cache/eslint/ .\",\n    \"format\": \"prettier --write --cache .\",\n    \"check\": \"node tools/checkAll.ts\",\n    \"test\": \"vitest run\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"0.21.0\",\n    \"@stdlib/math-base-special-cos\": \"0.3.1\",\n    \"@stdlib/math-base-special-pow\": \"0.3.1\",\n    \"@stdlib/math-base-special-sin\": \"0.3.1\",\n    \"three\": \"0.182.0\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk\": \"1.32.2\",\n    \"@playwright/test\": \"1.64.1\",\n    \"@stryker-mutator/core\": \"9.6.1\",\n    \"@stryker-mutator/vitest-runner\": \"9.6.1\",\n    \"@types/node\": \"24.19.2\",\n    \"@types/three\": \"0.182.0\",\n    \"eslint\": \"9.39.5\",\n    \"eslint-plugin-jsdoc\": \"61.7.1\",\n    \"globals\": \"16.5.0\",\n    \"prettier\": \"3.9.10\",\n    \"tsx\": \"4.23.15\",\n    \"typescript\": \"5.9.3\",\n    \"typescript-eslint\": \"8.72.1\",\n    \"vite\": \"7.3.7\",\n    \"vitest\": \"4.1.12\"\n  },\n  \"allowScripts\": {\n    \"esbuild\": false\n  }\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9dd98077-2f1f-4b79-bf36-2031f176f317","uuid":"c420d6d4-cb88-4cac-809b-190444b48914","timestamp":"2026-11-05T08:56:43.274Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Engine feedback: teeter\n\nWhat this game needed from the my-3dge engine (at 2dbd829) and did not get. The owner collects this file\nfrom every game to improve the engine, so write each item for an engine agent who has never seen this repository:\nwhat you tried, what happened (the command, the error code), what you expected, and what you did instead.\n\n## Requests\n\n<!-- What the engine should add or fix, most costly first. -->\n\n## Friction\n\n<!-- Docs, errors, templates or APIs that slowed you down or misled you, most costly first. -->\n\n## Local engine patches\n\n<!--\n`npm run check` accepts an edited engine file only when it is listed here, one line each, its path in backticks:\n- `engine/path/to/file.ts`: why no workaround in the game would do, and what the engine should change instead.\n-->\n@AGENTS.md\n\n# Claude Code notes\n\n- The SessionStart hook runs `bash scripts/setup.sh`; the after-edit hook formats and lints the file you edited:\n  fix what it reports.\n- When a replay misses its goldens or two runs part, docs/GAME-GUIDE.md section 7 says how to find why.\n# Agent-First Game Engine: Doctrine\n\n## How to read this\n\nRead this as a trusted, capable manager building an engine that you and others will use. We don't need to tell you to lock the door at night, keep company secrets off public message boards, avoid burning the place down, or get approval before spending money. Use your judgment. When you think going against this doctrine is best, escalate and explain your reasoning. Rules, principles need to be flexible and we have a process to allow for that.\n\n## North Star\n\nBuild a game engine designed from the ground up for AI coding agents (\"agents\"). Every lay