rows, bars and paging as well as lists. It reads the\n  same system layer, has hover focus and click to pick, and never shows more than six rows on a page.\n- **A seat-only bot.** `game.auto` runs the director too; `game.botSeat` (Dev Mode's \"Bot plays for me\") only plays your\n  seat, so the screens stay yours.\n- **The sky has no dome and the horizon has a haze ring.** A flat brochure background, a soft low-contrast sea, and a ring\n  past the fog so the world has no edge. The sea is unlit, so it does not catch the toon bands at grazing angles.\n- **Lines may run to five very short sentences**, as the plan's own seed lines do. The test holds them to that.\n- **Dev Mode's sliders and all settings** apply live and replays hold them. The plan's sway numbers stand as written;\n  the bots meet every target at defaults.\n- **Only CC0 clips were borrowed** (Mesh2Motion, Quaternius): no CMU or 100STYLE, so no extra licence terms to carry.\n- **Outward or costly things:** none were needed: no new dependency, no binary, nothing published. The engine is\n  unchanged. Git was left alone in this folder, as the plan says.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":40,"newStart":1,"newLines":74,"lines":["-# The starter game","+# Teeter Fit"," ","-Every game made with my-3dge starts as this one (`node tools/game/new-game.mjs`), then becomes its own: rewrite this","-page as the game grows. It is the game's page for the owner and the next agent: what the game is, how it plays, how to","-see it, and the calls made along the way.","+A balance-and-wellness game inspired by Wii Fit. You are a guest at the Little Fulcrum Wellness Retreat, a spotless","+island run by a retired bathroom scale who stopped weighing people and now measures only how much you wobble. Every","+activity (poses, reps, step class, paddling, a slackline, a tilting sand garden, sitting perfectly still while a frog","+judges you) is played through one verb: keep your centre of balance where it should be. Over five days the deadpan gets","+stranger, the plaques move, and on the last night the island tips and your thumb's balance is the island's."," ","-## The game","+## How it plays"," ","-Run round the arena collecting every gem before the clock runs out. WASD or the arrows run, Space jumps (over the low","-walls), Shift dashes. All the gems: won. The clock at 0: lost. Enter starts a new round on the next seed.","+**Lean** (left stick, WASD, or the mouse), **Left Foot** and **Right Foot** (L2 R2, Q E, or the mouse buttons), **Both","+Feet** to breathe (L2 + R2, Space, or both buttons). A first play takes about 45 minutes: five days, each a Morning Check","+and three activities, then the Great Tilt, a last check, credits, and Free Play for as long as you like."," ","+- Island: walk (left stick, WASD), look (right stick, mouse), use (Cross, E, or click the thing in view), hop (Square,","+  Space), the Programme (Triangle, Tab), wave (Circle, C). Every Programme item has Go, so nobody has to cross the island.","+- Pause: Options or Esc. Dev Mode is in the pause menu.","+- Group Class: up to four on one screen, CPU guests in the empty seats. In AI Hub: the same round as a ghost session.","+"," ## Files"," ","-| File                              | What it is                                                                    |","-| --------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |","-| labs/game/scenes/play.ts          | The rules, sim-side: the arena's text, the settings, the gems, the round, the systems |","-| labs/game/cast/player.ts          | The player: its character (run, jump, dash) and the body it is drawn as        |","-| labs/game/bot.ts                  | The bot: plays through intents, down the flow field to the nearest gem         |","-| labs/game/main.ts, index.html     | The page: the engine, the HUD, Enter to play again, the chase camera           |","-| labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts     | The tests: the golden replay, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds, the clock |","-| labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json | The bot's play from seed 1, with its golden hashes                             |","+| Where                          | What                                                                                   |","+| ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |","+| `scenes/island.ts`, `round.ts` | The two scenes: the whole game on one world, and the small session scene               |","+| `scenes/wobble.ts`             | The Wobble Model, the one verb                                                         |","+| `scenes/flow.ts`, `ending.ts`  | Starting, stepping and finishing activities; the story's one-way doors                 |","+| `scenes/events/*.ts`           | One module per activity, all the same shape, each with its own bot                     |","+| `scenes/bot/players.ts`        | The four players that stand in for a human: perfect, human, sloppy, idle               |","+| `scenes/data/*.ts`             | The tables: days, poses, charts, trays, courses, lures, stations, plaques. Add rows    |","+| `scenes/director.ts`           | The attract loop and `game.auto`'s scripted playthrough                                |","+| `cast/`                        | The clip set (strips scrubbed by your thumb), builds, the roster                       |","+| `props/`, `materials/`, `looks/`, `bodies/`, `emitters/`, `sounds/` | The art and the sound, all as data                            |","+| `cameras/shot.ts`, `ui/shots.ts` | One data-driven camera and the table of purpose-built shots                          |","+| `main.ts`, `ui/`               | The page: screens, HUD, input, Dev Mode, the Balance Disc                              |","+| `audio/`                       | The bus levels, the song choice, the gibberish voices                                  |","+| `hub/`                         | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode; the client kept byte-identical as text         |","+| `tools/`                       | `onefile.mjs` (the single HTML), `verify.mjs` (the one test command) and what it runs  |"," ","-## Settings","+## See it, check it"," ","-`game.gems` (10), `game.seconds` (30) and `game.speed` (6 m/s), read when the round starts: `?game.gems=20` in the","-page, `--set game.gems=20` for `node x sim` and `node x shot`.","-","-## See it","-"," ```sh","-npm run dev                                         # http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ (?replay=bot plays the bot's run)","-npm test                                            # the tests","-node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --feel          # the rules in Node: hash, events, how the player handles","-node x shot labs/game                               # the page on WebGPU, as numbers","-node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim       # the goldens, in Node and Chromium","+node labs/game/tools/onefile.mjs out/game.html        # the single HTML, runs from file://","+node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs                       # everything; prints only failures and one count line","+node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs --quick               # stops after the Node stages"," ```"," ","-## Decisions","+Set `CHROMIUM_PATH` to the headless shell for the browser stages. On a software renderer, `?gfx.warmUp=asked` boots","+sooner."," ","-<!-- The calls made without the owner, newest first: the date, what was decided, why. -->","+## The calls I made","+","+- **One camera mode, a table of shots.** The plan suggests a camera mode per activity. One data-driven `cam3=shot` that","+  the page aims each frame, easing towards the table in `ui/shots.ts`, makes every transition smooth and every activity's","+  camera one row. `cam3=chase` is kept for walking.","+- **A session spawns no props at all.** The engine makes a batch for every registered prop the moment the first prop","+  arrives, and on a slow GPU warming those takes 40 to 90 seconds, past the hub's 20. So `game:round` draws its floor and","+  mats as telegraphs, its pillars as stacks of plain blocks, and its ground as a small terrain; `main.ts` imports only the","+  scene it needs, so the island's props are not even registered in a session. A session boots in about 10 s on SwiftShader.","+- **Body-less props, posed directly.** The plan has things that move on kinematic bodies. Writing `sceneProp` x, y, z and","+  q works for a prop with no body, and needs no physics at all: the tray, marbles, board, boat and carts do this.","+- **Finale guests walk on the terrain by their own sums**, not as crowd agents in Rapier: deterministic, simple and no","+  stray bodies. They are still crowd bodies when drawn. The three named ones are the actors.","+- **One panel widget** instead of `createMenu`: Settings needs value rows, bars and paging as well as lists. It reads the","+  same system layer, has hover focus and click to pick, and never shows more than six rows on a page.","+- **A seat-only bot.** `game.auto` runs the director too; `game.botSeat` (Dev Mode's \"Bot plays for me\") only plays your","+  seat, so the screens stay yours.","+- **The sky has no dome and the horizon has a haze ring.** A flat brochure background, a soft low-contrast sea, and a ring","+  past the fog so the world has no edge. The sea is unlit, so it does not catch the toon bands at grazing angles.","+- **Lines may run to five very short sentences**, as the plan's own seed lines do. The test holds them to that.","+- **Dev Mode's sliders and all settings** apply live and replays hold them. The plan's sway numbers stand as written;","+  the bots meet every target at defaults.","+- **Only CC0 clips were borrowed** (Mesh2Motion, Quaternius): no CMU or 100STYLE, so no extra licence terms to carry.","+- **Outward or costly things:** none were needed: no new dependency, no binary, nothing published. The engine is","+  unchanged. Git was left alone in this folder, as the plan says."]}],"originalFile":"# The starter game\n\nEvery game made with my-3dge starts as this one (`node tools/game/new-game.mjs`), then becomes its own: rewrite this\npage as the game grows. It is the game's page for the owner and the next agent: what the game is, how it plays, how to\nsee it, and the calls made along the way.\n\n## The game\n\nRun round the arena collecting every gem before the clock runs out. WASD or the arrows run, Space jumps (over the low\nwalls), Shift dashes. All the gems: won. The clock at 0: lost. Enter starts a new round on the next seed.\n\n## Files\n\n| File                              | What it is                                                                    |\n| --------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| labs/game/scenes/play.ts          | The rules, sim-side: the arena's text, the settings, the gems, the round, the systems |\n| labs/game/cast/player.ts          | The player: its character (run, jump, dash) and the body it is drawn as        |\n| labs/game/bot.ts                  | The bot: plays through intents, down the flow field to the nearest gem         |\n| labs/game/main.ts, index.html     | The page: the engine, the HUD, Enter to play again, the chase camera           |\n| labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts     | The tests: the golden replay, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds, the clock |\n| labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json | The bot's play from seed 1, with its golden hashes                             |\n\n## Settings\n\n`game.gems` (10), `game.seconds` (30) and `game.speed` (6 m/s), read when the round starts: `?game.gems=20` in the\npage, `--set game.gems=20` for `node x sim` and `node x shot`.\n\n## See it\n\n```sh\nnpm run dev                                         # http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ (?replay=bot plays the bot's run)\nnpm test                                            # the tests\nnode x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --feel          # the rules in Node: hash, events, how the player handles\nnode x shot labs/game                               # the page on WebGPU, as numbers\nnode x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim       # the goldens, in Node and Chromium\n```\n\n## Decisions\n\n<!-- The calls made without the owner, newest first: the date, what was decided, why. -->\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HnoXRobiVFD6DsRXUu96G5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjhM6X7KCPHTvxDqDJRu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EeGNTaa8rVYsByrdqLWnRM","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-015601__x54facc/work/TeeterFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-015601/ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md","content":"# Engine feedback: teeter\n\nWhat this game needed from the my-3dge engine (at 2dbd829) and did not get. The owner collects this file\nfrom every game to improve the engine, so write each item for an engine agent who has never seen this repository:\nwhat you tried, what happened (the command, the error code), what you expected, and what you did instead.\n\n## Requests\n\n1. **Warm only the props a scene can spawn.** `createPropMeshes` makes a batch for every registered prop\n   (`engine/gfx/props.ts`: `options.props ?? registry.list('prop')`), and they all warm when the first prop arrives. On\n   SwiftShader a page with one prop spawned took 38 s with `gfx.warmUp=asked` and 89 s without; with none, 9 s. The AI Hub\n   allows 20 s. I expected a scene to warm what it uses. Instead the session scene spawns no prop at all, and imports only\n   its own scene's modules so the island's props are not registered. A `props: [...]` list on a scene, or warming a\n   prop's batches when its first instance arrives, would fix it.\n2. **Tint or colour an actor per instance.** A body's `slots` are a palette, but an actor can only name a whole body. Four\n   seat colours, six tracksuits and three looks became 48 `defineBody` calls in a loop. `spawnActor({ palette })`, read by\n   the view, would be one line.\n3. **A way to hide an actor or a prop without moving it.** \"Shown only while in the same area\" (hub ghosts) and \"hidden\n   until Day 4\" both became writing y = -30, with `placeActor`'s ceiling check to keep in mind.\n4. **A public `createRepeat`.** `engine/ui/widgets.ts` has the held-direction repeat every menu wants, but it is not in the\n   public API, so I wrote it again for a value-row panel.\n5. **A sea.** A huge flat prop as the plan describes shows dark mip-averaged banding near the horizon at grazing angles,\n   an edge where it ends, and a band below the horizon where nothing is drawn. A haze ring past the fog end and a softer\n   texture fixed it, after an hour. A `water` primitive that fades to the fog and has no edge would save every island.\n\n## Friction\n\n1. **Spawns inside `setup` and inside actions are queued to the boundary, and the failure is silent.** `w.spawn` returns an\n   id but `w.query` and `w.get` do not see it until the entry point ends. I looked people up by name in `setup`; every seat\n   got actor -1, and all poses silently did nothing. Tests passed, because scores do not need a body. It took a screenshot\n   to see. `w.get(id)` on a queued spawn could warn in a dev mode.\n2. **`inspector.describe('setting', path)` returns a list of `{ key, value, differs }`,** not the field's object. The plan\n   and the name suggest the object. My sliders wrote NaN (`CORE_BAD_SETTING`). A `field(path)` member would be clearer.\n3. **`defineActionMap` requires the four move actions** even for a menu map with nothing to move: `CORE_BAD_SPEC ... move\n   names \"forward\", which actions lacks`. Fine, but the message reads as if I named them.\n4. **`spawnProp` takes `{ prop, at, yaw }`.** The first guess `{ prop, position }` crashes in `propPose` with `Cannot read\n   properties of undefined (reading '0')`, far from the call. A checked placement would name the field.\n5. **`input.lastDevice` did not follow the mouse on hover or click in my tests,** so I tracked the device from the page's\n   own events. A note in its doc on when it changes would help.\n6. **Library ids clash without warning until load:** `prop:bench`, `sfx:whoosh`, `sfx:kick`. `CORE_DUPLICATE_ID` is clear,\n   but only at page load; `npm run check` passes. Reserving a game prefix, or checking in `x check`, would catch it sooner.\n7. **JSON imports fail the type check (TS6307)** though lint and Node accept them, and `tsconfig.*` is locked. I keep the\n   borrowed clips as a string constant parsed at load. A level-of-effort note in the guide would have saved the detour.\n8. **The guide's `labs/playground/...` paths are not in a game's workspace,** as the plan warned.\n9. **`erasableSyntaxOnly` refuses TypeScript parameter properties.** Fine, but it surfaces only at `tsc -b`, not in Vite's\n   build, so the page ran happily with code the check later refused.\n10. **`x shot`'s `LOOK_BLOWN` flags a pale sky** at 26%, for a look that means to be high-key. It is only advice, but there\n    is no way to accept it per look.\n\n## What worked well\n\nDeterminism across Node and Chromium held without any effort: replays agree first time. Headless `createHeadless` with\nscene actions made every activity testable by bot at step speed. The clip layer with hand-written strips scrubbed from\nthe sim was exactly as good as the plan said: a pose is one line of numbers, and it reads on screen.\n\n## Local engine patches\n\n<!--\n`npm run check` accepts an edited engine file only when it is listed here, one line each, its path in backticks:\n- `engine/path/to/file.ts`: why no workaround in the game would do, and what the engine should change instead.\n-->\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1284,"cache_read_input_tokens":790772,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1284},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9dd98077-2f1f-4b79-bf36-2031f176f317","uuid":"60ec39c5-2a39-4689-b27f-5b51eec08ffa","timestamp":"2026-11-05T11:24:43.519Z","request_id":"req_011CgjhM6B2NDRfcA6Yvvobx","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01EeGNTaa8rVYsByrdqLWnRM":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-015601__x54facc/work/TeeterFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-015601/ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md","content":"# Engine feedback: teeter\n\nWhat this game needed from the my-3dge engine (at 2dbd829) and did not get. The owner collects this file\nfrom every game to improve the engine, so write each item for an engine agent who has never seen this repository:\nwhat you tried, what happened (the command, the error code), what you expected, and what you did instead.\n\n## Requests\n\n1. **Warm only the props a scene can spawn.** `createPropMeshes` makes a batch for every registered prop\n   (`engine/gfx/props.ts`: `options.props ?? registry.list('prop')`), and they all warm when the first prop arrives. On\n   SwiftShader a page with one prop spawned took 38 s with `gfx.warmUp=asked` and 89 s without; with none, 9 s. The AI Hub\n   allows 20 s. I expected a scene to warm what it uses. Instead the session scene spawns no prop at all, and imports only\n   its own scene's modules so the island's props are not registered. A `props: [...]` list on a scene, or warming a\n   prop's batches when its first instance arrives, would fix it.\n2. **Tint or colour an actor per instance.** A body's `slots` are a palette, but an actor can only name a whole body. Four\n   seat colours, six tracksuits and three looks became 48 `defineBody` calls in a loop. `spawnActor({ palette })`, read by\n   the view, would be one line.\n3. **A way to hide an actor or a prop without moving it.** \"Shown only while in the same area\" (hub ghosts) and \"hidden\n   until Day 4\" both became writing y = -30, with `placeActor`'s ceiling check to keep in mind.\n4. **A public `createRepeat`.** `engine/ui/widgets.ts` has the held-direction repeat every menu wants, but it is not in the\n   public API, so I wrote it again for a value-row panel.\n5. **A sea.** A huge flat prop as the plan describes shows dark mip-averaged banding near the horizon at grazing angles,\n   an edge where it ends, and a band below the horizon where nothing is drawn. A haze ring past the fog end and a softer\n   texture fixed it, after an hour. A `water` primitive that fades to the fog and has no edge would save every island.\n\n## Friction\n\n1. **Spawns inside `setup` and inside actions are queued to the boundary, and the failure is silent.** `w.spawn` returns an\n   id but `w.query` and `w.get` do not see it until the entry point ends. I looked people up by name in `setup`; every seat\n   got actor -1, and all poses silently did nothing. Tests passed, because scores do not need a body. It took a screenshot\n   to see. `w.get(id)` on a queued spawn could warn in a dev mode.\n2. **`inspector.describe('setting', path)` returns a list of `{ key, value, differs }`,** not the field's object. The plan\n   and the name suggest the object. My sliders wrote NaN (`CORE_BAD_SETTING`). A `field(path)` member would be clearer.\n3. **`defineActionMap` requires the four move actions** even for a menu map with nothing to move: `CORE_BAD_SPEC ... move\n   names \"forward\", which actions lacks`. Fine, but the message reads as if I named them.\n4. **`spawnProp` takes `{ prop, at, yaw }`.** The first guess `{ prop, position }` crashes in `propPose` with `Cannot read\n   properties of undefined (reading '0')`, far from the call. A checked placement would name the field.\n5. **`input.lastDevice` did not follow the mouse on hover or click in my tests,** so I tracked the device from the page's\n   own events. A note in its doc on when it changes would help.\n6. **Library ids clash without warning until load:** `prop:bench`, `sfx:whoosh`, `sfx:kick`. `CORE_DUPLICATE_ID` is clear,\n   but only at page load; `npm run check` passes. Reserving a game prefix, or checking in `x check`, would catch it sooner.\n7. **JSON imports fail the type check (TS6307)** though lint and Node accept them, and `tsconfig.*` is locked. I keep the\n   borrowed clips as a string constant parsed at load. A level-of-effort note in the guide would have saved the detour.\n8. **The guide's `labs/playground/...` paths are not in a game's workspace,** as the plan warned.\n9. **`erasableSyntaxOnly` refuses TypeScript parameter properties.** Fine, but it surfaces only at `tsc -b`, not in Vite's\n   build, so the page ran happily with code the check later refused.\n10. **`x shot`'s `LOOK_BLOWN` flags a pale sky** at 26%, for a look that means to be high-key. It is only advice, but there\n    is no way to accept it per look.\n\n## What worked well\n\nDeterminism across Node and Chromium held without any effort: replays agree first time. Headless `createHeadless` with\nscene actions made every activity testable by bot at step speed. The clip layer with hand-written strips scrubbed from\nthe sim was exactly as good as the plan said: a pose is one line of numbers, and it reads on screen.\n\n## Local engine patches\n\n<!--\n`npm run check` accepts an edited engine file only when it is listed here, one line each, its path in backticks:\n- `engine/path/to/file.ts`: why no workaround in the game would do, and what the engine should change instead.\n-->\n"}}}
